The **Rule of Five** says that if a C++ class manually defines (or deletes) any one of these five special member functions, you should explicitly handle all five:

1. Destructor (`~Class()`)
2. Copy constructor (`Class(const Class&)`)
3. Copy assignment operator (`Class& operator=(const Class&)`)
4. Move constructor (`Class(Class&&)`)
5. Move assignment operator (`Class& operator=(Class&&)`)

**When you actually need to write them:** only when your class directly owns a raw resource—usually heap memory with a raw pointer (`new` / `delete`), or a file handle/socket. If you skip them, the default copy just copies the pointer address (a shallow copy), which leads to two objects freeing the same memory or leaking it.

```cpp
class Buffer {
    int* data;
public:
    Buffer(int n) : data(new int[n]) {}
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }
    // Must also define copy & move constructors/assignment here,
    // otherwise copying a Buffer leaves two objects pointing to the same `data`.
};
```

If your class only holds standard types like `std::string`, `std::vector`, or smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`), they already handle their own memory, so you write none of the five (**Rule of Zero**).

Quick check: if you have a `Course` class whose only members are `std::string title` and `std::vector<int> studentIds`, do you need to write a destructor or copy constructor for it?

